\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Faces, flags, and the symmetry of a shape}

\p{A cube turn refers to corners, edges, and a face containing them. Those
containments survive if we stretch the drawing. An abstract polytope retains
this incidence information without prescribing distances or angles. We use the
flag formulation of Schulte's axioms \citet{Section 2}{schulte2017regular}.
Throughout this section the rank #{n} is at least two.}

\subtree{
  \refcardt{Definition}{An abstract polytope}{Section 2}{schulte2017regular}{
    \p{An abstract #{n}-polytope is a partially ordered set #{P} of faces,
    with a least face #{F_{-1}} and greatest face #{F_n}. Every maximal chain,
    called a \em{flag}, contains exactly #{n+2} faces. The faces in a flag
    have ranks #{-1,0,\ldots,n}, consistently across flags. Faces other than
    the least and greatest are \em{proper}. Rank-zero faces are vertices;
    rank-one faces are edges; rank-#{n-1} faces are facets.}
    \p{The \em{diamond condition} says that if #{F<G} have ranks #{i-1}
    and #{i+1}, exactly two rank-#{i} faces lie strictly between them.
    Two flags are adjacent when they differ in exactly one proper face.
    \em{Strong flag connectivity} says that any two flags can be joined by
    adjacent flags, every one of which contains their intersection.}
    \p{For #{F\leq G}, the section #{G/F} is the interval
    #{\{H:F\leq H\leq G\}}. Its ranks are shifted so that its least face
    has rank #{-1}. If #{F,G} have ranks #{i,j}, this section has rank
    #{j-i-1}. The section #{F_n/v} at a vertex is its \em{vertex figure}.}
  }
}

\p{The connectivity condition must involve proper incidence or flags: allowing
the least face as an intermediate point would connect every pair for free.
The diamond condition makes a flag's neighbor at each rank unique. Neither
condition supplies a metric realization.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Example}
  \title{A triangle and a tetrahedron}
  \p{A triangle has the empty face, three vertices, three edges, and the whole
  triangle, of ranks #{-1,0,1,2}. One flag is
  #{\varnothing<1<12<123}. Its rank-zero neighbor uses vertex #{2}; its
  rank-one neighbor uses edge #{13}. Thus there are #{3\cdot2=6} flags.}
  \p{A tetrahedron has all subsets of #{\{1,2,3,4\}} as faces, ordered by
  inclusion; a nonempty face with #{k} vertices has rank #{k-1}. The flag
  #{\varnothing<1<12<123<1234} illustrates the five ranks. Its vertex
  figure at #{1} is a triangle: the three incident edges give the vertices
  of that figure. A cube also has a triangular vertex figure, although its
  facets are squares. Local and global shapes carry different information.}
}

\p{An automorphism is a bijection #{a:P\to P} such that
#{F\leq G} if and only if #{a(F)\leq a(G)}. Automorphisms form a group
#{\Gamma(P)} under composition; in #{ab}, the map #{b} acts first.
The polytope is \em{regular} when this group acts transitively on flags.
For the tetrahedron, a permutation of the four vertices sends any flag to
any other; it is therefore regular.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Lemma}
  \title{A flag determines an automorphism}
  \p{An automorphism fixing every face of one flag is the identity. Hence
  the action of a regular polytope's automorphism group on flags is free
  and transitive.}
  \proof{
    \p{Fix a flag pointwise. For an adjacent flag, only one rank has changed.
    The unchanged neighboring ranks bound a diamond with two possible faces.
    Since one is already fixed, the other must be fixed too. The adjacent
    flag is therefore fixed pointwise. Propagating along the connected flag
    graph fixes every flag, and every face belongs to a flag. Thus every
    face is fixed. Transitivity is the definition of regularity.}
  }
}

\p{Choose a base flag in a regular #{n}-polytope. Let #{\rho_i} be the unique
automorphism taking it to its rank-#{i} neighbor, for #{0\leq i<n}.
Uniqueness and the diamond condition give #{\rho_i^2=1}. The generators
satisfy #{\rho_i\rho_j=\rho_j\rho_i} when #{|i-j|>1}: the two changes
affect independent entries of the flag, and the resulting automorphisms agree
on that flag. Connectivity shows that these generators reach all flags and
therefore generate #{\Gamma(P)}. For #{I\subseteq\{0,\ldots,n-1\}}, write
#{G_I=\langle\rho_i:i\in I\rangle}. These subgroups satisfy the
\em{intersection property}
##{G_I\cap G_J=G_{I\cap J}.}
This is the additional condition in the associated string C-group description
\citet{Section 5, equation (18)}{schulte2017regular}. We use the
face-poset axioms here and do not assume that arbitrary involutions with the
commutation relations reconstruct a polytope
\citet{Section 3}{schulte2017regular}.}

\subtree{
  \taxon{Lemma}
  \title{Extending a symmetry of one facet}
  \p{For a facet #{H} of a regular polytope, restriction gives an isomorphism
  from its stabilizer in #{\Gamma(P)} to #{\Gamma(H/F_{-1})}.}
  \proof{
    \p{Extend a flag of the facet by the greatest face of #{P}. If a
    stabilizer element restricts to the identity, it fixes this whole flag,
    hence is the identity. For surjectivity, let #{\alpha} be a facet
    automorphism. Regularity supplies an automorphism of #{P} taking the
    extended flag to the extension of its image under #{\alpha}. It fixes
    #{H}; its restriction agrees with #{\alpha} on the chosen facet flag.
    The previous lemma, applied to the facet, makes the restrictions equal.
    The same argument shows that the facet itself is regular.}
  }
}

\p{This extension result is the bridge to a move: a symmetry of a facet can
act consistently on the whole incidence structure. A puzzle move will use
that symmetry only on pieces whose locations belong to the chosen facet.
This restriction is why a shape symmetry and a puzzle move are different
permutations.}

\p{For a concrete preview, take the tetrahedron's facet #{H=123}. Its
rotation #{(123)} extends to the whole tetrahedron by fixing vertex #{4}.
Label an incidence record by a vertex and an incident edge, such as
#{(1,12)}. Apply the extension to both entries only when the first vertex
lies in #{H}. Then
##{(1,12)\longmapsto(2,23)\longmapsto(3,13)\longmapsto(1,12).}
The record #{(4,14)} stays fixed under this local move, although the global
shape symmetry sends it to #{(4,24)}. The next section defines the general
action and proves that it is a permutation of the chosen incidence data.}
